Our order-status webhook handler intermittently drops updates — maybe 1 in 50 batches, an order stays in the old status and nothing appears in the error logs. Node 20, Express 4; the process is started with `--unhandled-rejections=warn` (a legacy flag from an old incident). Here's the handler:

```js
app.post('/webhooks/orders', async (req, res) => {
  const { events } = req.body;
  events.forEach(async (evt) => {
    await db.orders.updateStatus(evt.orderId, evt.status);
    await notifySlack(evt);
  });
  res.sendStatus(200);
});
```

`db.orders.updateStatus` and `notifySlack` both return promises and can throw (Slack rate-limits us sometimes). The webhook provider retries on any non-2xx response. Can you explain why updates go missing with no log trace, and fix the handler? We deploy this service on a single small container, so keep the change contained to this route.

We're adding a fourth subscription tier next sprint and this pricing function is already painful — three copy-pasted branches that have drifted before (the annual discount was once fixed in two branches but not the third, which caused a billing incident). Refactor it so adding a tier is a one-place change, with identical behavior for all current inputs. TypeScript, no new dependencies.

```ts
function computeMonthlyPrice(plan: string, seats: number, annual: boolean): number {
  if (plan === "starter") {
    let base = 9 * seats;
    if (seats > 10) base = base * 0.9;
    if (annual) base = base * 0.85;
    return Math.round(base * 100) / 100;
  } else if (plan === "growth") {
    let base = 29 * seats;
    if (seats > 25) base = base * 0.85;
    if (annual) base = base * 0.85;
    return Math.round(base * 100) / 100;
  } else if (plan === "enterprise") {
    let base = 79 * seats;
    if (seats > 100) base = base * 0.8;
    if (annual) base = base * 0.85;
    return Math.round(base * 100) / 100;
  }
  throw new Error(`unknown plan: ${plan}`);
}
```

